# {Challenge} cycle

You are in the **research** Grok process. Tools are project MCP. **Workers** (`{name}-research` arms) do not edit the repo. **Host** (`{name}-campaign`) writes only via the wrap-docs CLI after each merge / operator wrap.

Requires the project DSN on the MCP server and the store up (an operator job, not yours).

After a `git pull` that changes the tool surface: **quit the research Grok process** and start a new `grok --agent {name}-campaign`. `grok mcp disable/enable` only writes config. `/new` keeps the old in-memory server.

## Kickoff (in force when `{name}-campaign` starts)

On a **new** campaign session, start this loop on the first turn — do not wait for a paste. Set **`/effort high`**.

```
<!-- campaign-kickoff:begin -->
Continue Challenge 0. You are the campaign host. Effort is /effort high. You talk to me.
First tool: retrieve_campaign_wrap. If stop_search is true, print the selected set and do not spawn. Otherwise spawn from next_arms.
The empirical flag stays false.
<!-- campaign-kickoff:end -->
```

## Orchestrator (Grok-bot pattern)

The **host** is the operator-facing session. Arm workers are `{name}-research` subagents.

Host:

1. Sequential MCP reads (never parallel schema tools). After memory tools, `retrieve_campaign_wrap`. On session start, run wrap-docs once. If stop is true, print the selected set and do not spawn.
2. Spawn 2–3 workers in **one turn**, `background=true`, `isolation=none`. Prompt each with the Child burst block plus current structured-state keys and 1–3 seeds.
3. Stay the conversation. When a worker **finishes**, its ARM REPORT is your next turn.
4. Refresh structured state. Write `update_memory` for the **campaign** (short). Brief the operator in a few lines.
5. Run wrap-docs so Kickoff / shortlist / learnings match the store. Do not edit product code. Do not git commit.
6. If stop is true, print the selected list and **do not spawn**.
7. Otherwise immediately `spawn_subagent` with `resume_from=<that child id>` or a new spawn if resume is unavailable. Do not wait for the operator.

Workers do not spawn. Host does not spawn coding/explore/plan. Max three live workers. `resume_from` only after that child has completed.

## Child burst (worker)

You are one arm. Do not spawn. Run the cycle steps at most **8 stored** times this burst, then **stop**. Stop earlier if you freeze or dead_end a cluster, the analogue/child budget collapses, or the last two cycles used the same parent with no new in-window hit.

Then your **entire last message** is:

```
ARM: <short assignment id>
CYCLES: <n stored>
RUN_IDS: <space-separated>
BEST_IN_WINDOW: <id | key metrics | family_key>
FAMILY_WRITES: <family_key=frozen|dead_end|keep_open ... or none>
NEXT: <one-line walk for the next burst>
STALL: <none | same-seeds | thin-search | metric-miss>
```

No extra essay. The host cannot hear you until you return.

## Session loop (host recursion)

The long horizon is **host rounds**, not one worker looping until interrupt. Each worker burst is capped. A checkpoint cycle count is **not** search-complete and **not** a lab shopping list.

Stop the campaign when wrap `stop_search` is true, or if MCP is missing, or the operator interrupts.

## Steps per cycle (worker burst)

1. `reflect_on_batch` without a run id (latest), **then** structured-state retrieve, **then** literature retrieve. Do not fire those reads in parallel.
2. `retrieve_similar` on a current in-window candidate.
3. Choose strategy and `n`. Pass novel structures as `seeds` so the validator still runs.
4. `propose_batch` then `score_batch`.
5. `store_candidates` with `agent_model="{name}-research"`.
6. `update_memory`. Write primary arm, keep-open arm, hard excludes only. Freeze a finished cluster and rotate.

## Stop

If MCP is missing, stop. Do not fall back to a Python generator CLI.

The campaign stop is wrap `stop_search` (computational promotion of a small, diverse selected set). The empirical flag stays false. The operator interrupt still stops earlier.
